    {
      "time": 1789570250.1277773,
      "raw_time": false,
      "id": "ae620908-e161-41bb-b95c-b04d3fa65045",
      "sender": "ahn",
      "type": "msg",
      "metadata": {
        "model": {
          "id": null,
          "settings": {}
        },
        "to_persona": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
        "settings": {
          "__mode__": "plan"
        }
      },
      "body": "I have an error in 24th cell of baseline template"
    },
    {
      "mentions": [],
      "type": "msg",
      "body": "",
      "sender": "jupyter-ai-personas::jupyter_ai_acp_client::ClaudeAcpPersona",
      "id": "4742e905-d8db-41fd-bbae-6f268582f702",
      "metadata": {
        "tool_calls": [
          {
            "raw_input": {
              "notebook_path": "adi_wdir/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/sf_triggers/1_baseline_template.ipynb"
            },
            "status": "completed",
            "tool_call_id": "toolu_bdrk_01DcCuAYnnMabepcihrrmgtp",
            "title": "mcp__Jupyter_MCP_Server__read_notebook_cells",
            "raw_output": "{\"result\":[{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"# PyTorch Lightning baseline template\\n\\nby Andr\u00e9s Mu\u00f1oz-Jaramillo\\n\\nThis notebook is meant to act as a template to train and use a simple regression model to define a baseline that can be compared with a DS application.\\n\\nIt focuses on the concept of defining a PyTorch model, a PyTorch lightning training loop and the deffinition of metrics of performance.\\n\\nThis notebook assumes familiarity with the concepts of datasets and dataloaders contained in the **_0_dataset_dataloader_template.ipynb_**\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"d651fb69\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"## Set your cuda visible device\\n\\n**IMPORTANT:** Since we are sharing resources, please make sure that the cuda visible device you put here is the one assigned to your team and your machine.   \",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"f6bade9c\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"import os\\nos.environ[\\\"CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES\\\"] = \\\"0\\\"\",\"execution_count\":1,\"cell_id\":\"6d6af87a\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"# Must be set BEFORE torch is imported: cuBLAS reads this once, when it initializes, so\\n# setting it later has no effect. It is what lets training.deterministic work without a\\n# cuBLAS warning on every run. (Restart the kernel if torch was already imported.)\\nimport os\\nos.environ.setdefault(\\\"CUBLAS_WORKSPACE_CONFIG\\\", \\\":4096:8\\\")\\n\\nimport sys\\nfrom torch.utils.data import DataLoader\\n\\nimport torch\\nimport yaml\\n\\nimport lightning as L\\nfrom lightning.pytorch.callbacks import ModelCheckpoint\\nfrom lightning.pytorch.loggers import CSVLogger, WandbLogger\\n\\n# Append base path.  May need to be modified if the folder structure changes.\\n# It gives the notebook access to the wokshop_infrastructure folder.\\nsys.path.append(\\\"../../\\\")\\n \\n# Append Surya path. May need to be modified if the folder structure changes.\\n# It gives the notebook access to surya's release code.\\n\\nfrom workshop_infrastructure.utils import build_scalers  # Data scaling utilities for Surya stacks\\n\\ntorch.set_float32_matmul_precision('medium')\\n\\n\",\"execution_count\":2,\"cell_id\":\"4ed04321\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"## Load configuration\\n\\nSurya was designed to read a configuration file that defines many aspects of the model\\nincluding the data it uses we use this config file to set default values that do not\\nneed to be modified, but also to define values specific to our downstream application\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"04135ff7\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"# The config is the single source of truth. load_flare_config() parses it into a typed\\n# object, exactly as the training script 3_finetune_template_1D.py does, so the same YAML\\n# behaves identically here and in production. Notebook 0 walks through what it contains.\\nfrom downstream_apps.sf_triggers.configs import load_flare_config\\n\\ncfg = load_flare_config(\\\"./configs/config_script.yaml\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"Loaded config for job: {cfg.job_id}\\\")\\n\",\"execution_count\":3,\"cell_id\":\"63c1fe60\",\"language\":\"python\",\"outputs\":[{\"output_type\":\"stream\",\"text\":\"Loaded config for job: solar_flare_forecasting\\n\"}]},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"## Download assets\\n\\nThe config says where the assets belong, so it is loaded first. `ensure_assets()` fetches only what is missing from HuggingFace, so re-running this is free.\\n\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"1f639975\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"# One implementation, shared by the notebooks, the training script and the\\n# download_*.sh wrappers: workshop_infrastructure/assets.py.\\n# The linear baseline needs no backbone, so skip the 1.8 GB weights.\\nfrom workshop_infrastructure.assets import ensure_assets\\n\\nensure_assets(cfg, which=[\\\"scalers\\\"])\\n\\n# Now that scalers.yaml is guaranteed to be on disk, load it. build_scalers()\\n# accepts the resolved path directly.\\nscalers = build_scalers(info=cfg.data.scalers_path)\\nprint(f\\\"Loaded scalers for {len(scalers)} channels.\\\")\\n\",\"execution_count\":4,\"cell_id\":\"c74728d9\",\"language\":\"python\",\"outputs\":[{\"output_type\":\"stream\",\"text\":\"Loaded scalers for 13 channels.\\n\"}]},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"## Define Downstream (DS) datasets\\n\\nThis child class takes as input all expected HelioFM parameters, plus additonal parameters relevant to the downstream application.  Here we focus in particular to the DS index and parameters necessary to combine it with the HelioFM index.\\n\\nAnother important component of creating a dataset class for your DS is normalization.  Here we use a log normalization on xray flux that will act as the output target.  Making log10(xray_flux) strictly positive and having 66% of its values between 0 and 1\\n\\nIn this case we will define both a training and a validation dataset using the indices pointed at in the config\\n\\n**_Important:  In this notebook we sets max_number_of_samples=6 to potentially avoid going through the whole dataset as we explore it.  Keep in mind this for the future in case the database seems smaller than you expect_**\\n\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"af6574dc\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"from downstream_apps.sf_triggers.datasets.sf_triggers_dataset import FlareDSDataset\",\"execution_count\":5,\"cell_id\":\"34db2cc6\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"from workshop_infrastructure.datasets.builders import build_helio_dataloaders\\n\\ntrain_data_loader, val_data_loader = build_helio_dataloaders(\\n    cfg,\\n    FlareDSDataset,\\n    scalers=scalers,\\n    num_workers=4,          # fewer workers than the script: notebooks start faster\\n    #### Downstream (DS) specific parameters\\n    return_surya_stack=True,\\n    max_number_of_samples=6,\\n    ds_flare_index_path=cfg.data.flare_index_path,\\n    ds_time_column=cfg.data.ds_time_column,\\n    ds_time_tolerance=cfg.data.ds_time_tolerance,\\n    ds_match_direction=cfg.data.ds_match_direction,\\n    ds_val_fraction=cfg.data.ds_val_fraction,\\n    ds_split_seed=cfg.data.ds_split_seed,\\n    mask_dir=cfg.data.mask_dir,\\n    mask_time_tolerance=cfg.data.mask_time_tolerance,\\n)\\n\\nbatch_size = cfg.batch_size\\nprint(f\\\"train: {len(train_data_loader.dataset)} samples | \\\"\\n      f\\\"val: {len(val_data_loader.dataset)} samples | batch_size: {batch_size}\\\")\\n\",\"execution_count\":6,\"cell_id\":\"70e9c5b2\",\"language\":\"python\",\"outputs\":[{\"output_type\":\"stream\",\"text\":\"train: 6 samples | val: 2 samples | batch_size: 2\\n\"}]},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"Training and validation get separate datasets and dataloaders. They differ only in the index they read and in `phase`: `phase=\\\"val\\\"` turns off the random channel masking and vertical flips used for training augmentation.\\n\\nThe loaders use `multiprocessing_context=\\\"spawn\\\"` \u2014 the dataset holds an S3 client that does not survive `fork`, and spawn also avoids lockups in shared environments.\\n\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"79fda7cf\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"# Inspect a single batch to confirm shapes before training.\\nbatch = next(iter(train_data_loader))\\nprint({k: (tuple(v.shape) if hasattr(v, \\\"shape\\\") else type(v).__name__) for k, v in batch.items()})\\n\",\"execution_count\":7,\"cell_id\":\"48bb2912\",\"language\":\"python\",\"outputs\":[{\"output_type\":\"stream\",\"text\":\"{'ts': (2, 13, 1, 4096, 4096), 'time_delta_input': (2, 1), 'forecast': (2, 1, 4096, 4096), 'ds_index': 'list'}\\n\"}]},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"## Define simple baseline model\\n\\nDefining a simple baseline is important to understand what value is bringing the AI model to the problem.  \\n\\nIt is always very good to have a very simple baseline model.  Ideally one that cannot overfit the data.  This is a very good way of really measuring the value added of complex models.   Classical machine learning excels here:\\n\\n- Regressions and logistic regressions.\\n- Climatological averages.\\n- Persistance.\\n- Simple transformations.\\n\\nSimple models avoid excesively optimistic assessments of the capatiblities of a complex models and for many problems are actually remarkably hard to beat.\\n\\nIn this example we define a simple regression acting on the intensity of each channel.  Note that we invert the normalization to deal with strictly positive quantities.  As with the dataset we will be importing the model from a module so that we can use it within training scripts later on.\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"1d4d2dea\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"from downstream_apps.sf_triggers.models.simple_baseline import RegressionFlareModel\",\"execution_count\":8,\"cell_id\":\"3f5fd639\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"We can now test that this model manipulates a batch as expected and returns an estimate of flare intensity\\n\\nNote that the simple regression model definition requires knowing the number of channels and timesteps so here we pull that information from the configuration intializing the model.\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"18d87992\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"n_input_timestamps = cfg.model.time_embedding.time_dim\\nn_channels = len(cfg.data.channels)\\n\\n# RegressionFlareModel only needs the flattened input dimension.\\n# It expects 'ts' in signum-log space \u2014 use destandardize_channels() before calling forward().\\nmodel = RegressionFlareModel(n_input_timestamps * n_channels)\\n\",\"execution_count\":9,\"cell_id\":\"b0b6ce02\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"Now we can pass the input stack 'ts' to the model to transform it into our regression output.   Note that since this model has not been trained and was initalized randomly.  The output here has no real meaning.  It only acts as a test that our model forward doesn't have dimension problems.\\n\\nDimension problemns are the dominant source of error in this kind of work.\\n\\nNote that our output has now the size of our batch.\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"3fee1e68\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"from downstream_apps.sf_triggers.models.simple_baseline import destandardize_channels\\n\\nbatch = next(iter(train_data_loader))\\n\\n# RegressionFlareModel works in signum-log space, not normalized space.\\n# destandardize_channels undoes the per-channel z-score but KEEPS the log compression \u2014\\n# raw DN values span too many orders of magnitude to be good features for one linear layer.\\n# (For true physical units, use train_dataset.inverse_transform_data() instead. See the\\n#  \\\"THE THREE SPACES\\\" block in workshop_infrastructure/datasets/helio.py.)\\nbatch_logspace = destandardize_channels(batch, channel_order=cfg.data.channels, scalers=scalers)\\noutput = model.forward(batch_logspace)[:, 0]  # Get rid of singleton dimension\\noutput\\n\",\"execution_count\":11,\"cell_id\":\"d7d97841\",\"language\":\"python\",\"outputs\":[{\"output_type\":\"execute_result\",\"text\":\"tensor([0.3029, 0.2948], grad_fn=<SelectBackward0>)\",\"image\":null}]},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"## Define your metrics\\n\\nMetrics are a very important part of training AI models.   They provide your models with the quantitification of error, which in turn shifts the weights towards better pefrorming models.  They also provide a way for you to monitor performance, identify overfitting, and quantify value added. \\n\\nWe now initialize the metrics class which allows you to control what metrics do you want to use as \\\"loss\\\" (i.e. the metrics that backpropagate through your model) and which ones for monitoring performance.  As with other components, this takes the form of a loaded module that can be later use in a training script\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"36f32e64\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"from downstream_apps.sf_triggers.metrics.sf_triggers_metrics import FlareMetrics\",\"execution_count\":15,\"cell_id\":\"34c502ec\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"train_loss_metrics = FlareMetrics(\\\"train_loss\\\")\\n# val_loss is the quantity logged as \\\"val_loss\\\" and used to pick the best checkpoint.\\n# It defaults to the same MSE as train_loss \u2014 override FlareMetrics.val_loss to change it.\\nval_loss_metrics = FlareMetrics(\\\"val_loss\\\")\\ntrain_evaluation_metrics = FlareMetrics(\\\"train_metrics\\\")\\n# Reported only: val_metrics do NOT influence checkpoint selection.\\nvalidation_evaluation_metrics = FlareMetrics(\\\"val_metrics\\\")\",\"execution_count\":18,\"cell_id\":\"d152b127\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"Now they can be evaluated in our model's output and our ground truth.   First the loss that actually will backpropagate, in this case Mean Squared Errror\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"0550062c\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"train_loss_metrics(output, batch[\\\"forecast\\\"])\",\"execution_count\":19,\"cell_id\":\"2baf9d36\",\"language\":\"python\",\"outputs\":[{\"output_type\":\"error\",\"text\":\"RuntimeError: The size of tensor a (2) must match the size of tensor b (33554432) at non-singleton dimension 0\\n\\u001b[0;31m---------------------------------------------------------------------------\\u001b[0m\\n\\u001b[0;31mRuntimeError\\u001b[0m                              Traceback (most recent call last)\\nCell \\u001b[0;32mIn[19], line 1\\u001b[0m\\n\\u001b[0;32m----> 1\\u001b[0m \\u001b[43mtrain_loss_metrics\\u001b[49m\\u001b[43m(\\u001b[49m\\u001b[43moutput\\u001b[49m\\u001b[43m,\\u001b[49m\\u001b[43m \\u001b[49m\\u001b[43mbatch\\u001b[49m\\u001b[43m[\\u001b[49m\\u001b[38;5;124;43m\\\"\\u001b[39;49m\\u001b[38;5;124;43mforecast\\u001b[39;49m\\u001b[38;5;124;43m\\\"\\u001b[39;49m\\u001b[43m]\\u001b[49m\\u001b[43m)\\u001b[49m\\n\\nFile \\u001b[0;32m~/adi_wdir/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/sf_triggers/../../downstream_apps/sf_triggers/metrics/sf_triggers_metrics.py:175\\u001b[0m, in \\u001b[0;36mFlareMetrics.__call__\\u001b[0;34m(self, preds, target)\\u001b[0m\\n\\u001b[1;32m    172\\u001b[0m \\u001b[38;5;28;01mmatch\\u001b[39;00m \\u001b[38;5;28mself\\u001b[39m\\u001b[38;5;241m.\\u001b[39mmode\\u001b[38;5;241m.\\u001b[39mlower():\\n\\u001b[1;32m    174\\u001b[0m     \\u001b[38;5;28;01mcase\\u001b[39;00m \\u001b[38;5;124m\\\"\\u001b[39m\\u001b[38;5;124mtrain_loss\\u001b[39m\\u001b[38;5;124m\\\"\\u001b[39m:\\n\\u001b[0;32m--> 175\\u001b[0m         \\u001b[38;5;28;01mreturn\\u001b[39;00m \\u001b[38;5;28;43mself\\u001b[39;49m\\u001b[38;5;241;43m.\\u001b[39;49m\\u001b[43mtrain_loss\\u001b[49m\\u001b[43m(\\u001b[49m\\u001b[43mpreds\\u001b[49m\\u001b[43m,\\u001b[49m\\u001b[43m \\u001b[49m\\u001b[43mtarget\\u001b[49m\\u001b[43m)\\u001b[49m\\n\\u001b[1;32m    177\\u001b[0m     \\u001b[38;5;66;03m# No torch.no_grad() here, matching \\\"train_loss\\\": Lightning already disables\\u001b[39;00m\\n\\u001b[1;32m    178\\u001b[0m     \\u001b[38;5;66;03m# gradients during validation, so wrapping it would differ gratuitously from\\u001b[39;00m\\n\\u001b[1;32m    179\\u001b[0m     \\u001b[38;5;66;03m# the loss case this mirrors.\\u001b[39;00m\\n\\u001b[1;32m    180\\u001b[0m     \\u001b[38;5;28;01mcase\\u001b[39;00m \\u001b[38;5;124m\\\"\\u001b[39m\\u001b[38;5;124mval_loss\\u001b[39m\\u001b[38;5;124m\\\"\\u001b[39m:\\n\\nFile \\u001b[0;32m~/adi_wdir/surya_workshop/downstream_apps/sf_triggers/../../downstream_apps/sf_triggers/metrics/sf_triggers_metrics.py:65\\u001b[0m, in \\u001b[0;36mFlareMetrics.train_loss\\u001b[0;34m(self, preds, target)\\u001b[0m\\n\\u001b[1;32m     62\\u001b[0m output_metrics \\u001b[38;5;241m=\\u001b[39m {}\\n\\u001b[1;32m     63\\u001b[0m output_weights \\u001b[38;5;241m=\\u001b[39m []\\n\\u001b[0;32m---> 65\\u001b[0m output_metrics[\\u001b[38;5;124m\\\"\\u001b[39m\\u001b[38;5;124mmse\\u001b[39m\\u001b[38;5;124m\\\"\\u001b[39m] \\u001b[38;5;241m=\\u001b[39m \\u001b[43mtorch\\u001b[49m\\u001b[38;5;241;43m.\\u001b[39;49m\\u001b[43mnn\\u001b[49m\\u001b[38;5;241;43m.\\u001b[39;49m\\u001b[43mfunctional\\u001b[49m\\u001b[38;5;241;43m.\\u001b[39;49m\\u001b[43mmse_loss\\u001b[49m\\u001b[43m(\\u001b[49m\\u001b[43mpreds\\u001b[49m\\u001b[38;5;241;43m.\\u001b[39;49m\\u001b[43mreshape\\u001b[49m\\u001b[43m(\\u001b[49m\\u001b[38;5;241;43m-\\u001b[39;49m\\u001b[38;5;241;43m1\\u001b[39;49m\\u001b[43m)\\u001b[49m\\u001b[43m,\\u001b[49m\\u001b[43m \\u001b[49m\\u001b[43mtarget\\u001b[49m\\u001b[38;5;241;43m.\\u001b[39;49m\\u001b[43mreshape\\u001b[49m\\u001b[43m(\\u001b[49m\\u001b[38;5;241;43m-\\u001b[39;49m\\u001b[38;5;241;43m1\\u001b[39;49m\\u001b[43m)\\u001b[49m\\u001b[43m)\\u001b[49m\\n\\u001b[1;32m     66\\u001b[0m output_weights\\u001b[38;5;241m.\\u001b[39mappend(\\u001b[38;5;241m1\\u001b[39m)\\n\\u001b[1;32m     68\\u001b[0m \\u001b[38;5;28;01mreturn\\u001b[39;00m output_metrics, output_weights\\n\\nFile \\u001b[0;32m~/envs/surya_ws/lib/python3.12/site-packages/torch/nn/functional.py:3864\\u001b[0m, in \\u001b[0;36mmse_loss\\u001b[0;34m(input, target, size_average, reduce, reduction, weight)\\u001b[0m\\n\\u001b[1;32m   3861\\u001b[0m \\u001b[38;5;28;01mif\\u001b[39;00m size_average \\u001b[38;5;129;01mis\\u001b[39;00m \\u001b[38;5;129;01mnot\\u001b[39;00m \\u001b[38;5;28;01mNone\\u001b[39;00m \\u001b[38;5;129;01mor\\u001b[39;00m reduce \\u001b[38;5;129;01mis\\u001b[39;00m \\u001b[38;5;129;01mnot\\u001b[39;00m \\u001b[38;5;28;01mNone\\u001b[39;00m:\\n\\u001b[1;32m   3862\\u001b[0m     reduction \\u001b[38;5;241m=\\u001b[39m _Reduction\\u001b[38;5;241m.\\u001b[39mlegacy_get_string(size_average, reduce)\\n\\u001b[0;32m-> 3864\\u001b[0m expanded_input, expanded_target \\u001b[38;5;241m=\\u001b[39m \\u001b[43mtorch\\u001b[49m\\u001b[38;5;241;43m.\\u001b[39;49m\\u001b[43mbroadcast_tensors\\u001b[49m\\u001b[43m(\\u001b[49m\\u001b[38;5;28;43minput\\u001b[39;49m\\u001b[43m,\\u001b[49m\\u001b[43m \\u001b[49m\\u001b[43mtarget\\u001b[49m\\u001b[43m)\\u001b[49m\\n\\u001b[1;32m   3866\\u001b[0m \\u001b[38;5;28;01mif\\u001b[39;00m weight \\u001b[38;5;129;01mis\\u001b[39;00m \\u001b[38;5;129;01mnot\\u001b[39;00m \\u001b[38;5;28;01mNone\\u001b[39;00m:\\n\\u001b[1;32m   3867\\u001b[0m     \\u001b[38;5;28;01mif\\u001b[39;00m weight\\u001b[38;5;241m.\\u001b[39msize() \\u001b[38;5;241m!=\\u001b[39m \\u001b[38;5;28minput\\u001b[39m\\u001b[38;5;241m.\\u001b[39msize():\\n\\nFile \\u001b[0;32m~/envs/surya_ws/lib/python3.12/site-packages/torch/functional.py:77\\u001b[0m, in \\u001b[0;36mbroadcast_tensors\\u001b[0;34m(*tensors)\\u001b[0m\\n\\u001b[1;32m     75\\u001b[0m \\u001b[38;5;28;01mif\\u001b[39;00m has_torch_function(tensors):\\n\\u001b[1;32m     76\\u001b[0m     \\u001b[38;5;28;01mreturn\\u001b[39;00m handle_torch_function(broadcast_tensors, tensors, \\u001b[38;5;241m*\\u001b[39mtensors)\\n\\u001b[0;32m---> 77\\u001b[0m \\u001b[38;5;28;01mreturn\\u001b[39;00m \\u001b[43m_VF\\u001b[49m\\u001b[38;5;241;43m.\\u001b[39;49m\\u001b[43mbroadcast_tensors\\u001b[49m\\u001b[43m(\\u001b[49m\\u001b[43mtensors\\u001b[49m\\u001b[43m)\\u001b[49m\\n\\n\\u001b[0;31mRuntimeError\\u001b[0m: The size of tensor a (2) must match the size of tensor b (33554432) at non-singleton dimension 0\"}]},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"Then a training evaluation that will not backpropagate and inform our model, but that we can keep an eye on. Note that reporting lots of metrics during training will slow the training process.  I'm including it her as an example, but oftentimes is better to put the diagnostics only in the validation evaluation metrics.\\n\\nHere we are caclulating the Root Relative Squared Error https://lightning.ai/docs/torchmetrics/stable/regression/rse.html \\n\\nA value below one means the prediction is better than predicting the average.  It is unlikely that this metric will be lower than one with a randomly initialized model\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"3dc5eb6d\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"train_evaluation_metrics(output, batch[\\\"forecast\\\"])\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"796559f1\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"In the validation evaluation metrics we report both MSE and RRSE\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"3d65e7cd\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"validation_evaluation_metrics(output, batch[\\\"forecast\\\"])\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"0a0bbb35\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"## Define your PyTorch ligthning module\\n\\nIn this workshop we will use PyTorch lightning to train our models.  PyTorch lighting reduces the amount of code required to implement a training loop in comparison to PyTorch (at the expense of control and versatility).  \\n\\nOpening the FlareLightningModule shows a simple Lightning model implementation.  It consists of:\\n\\n- An initialization of the class (metrics, model, and learning rate).\\n- The forward code that runs evaluation of the model.\\n- Training and validation steps.\\n- Configuration of optimizers.\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"537871ed\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"from downstream_apps.sf_triggers.lightning_modules.pl_simple_baseline import FlareLightningModule\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"9d730cf3\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"## Set your global seeds\\n\\nSince training AI models generally uses stochastic gradient descent, it is a good idea to fix your random seeds so that your training exercise is reproducible.    \",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"3321963d\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"L.seed_everything(42, workers=True)\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"cc28903d\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"## Intialize Lightning module\\n\\nNow we properly initalize the Lightning module to enable training, including passing the dictionary of metrics\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"119ec413\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"from functools import partial\\n\\nmetrics = {\\n    'train_loss': train_loss_metrics,\\n    'val_loss': val_loss_metrics,\\n    'train_metrics': train_evaluation_metrics,\\n    'val_metrics': validation_evaluation_metrics,\\n}\\n\\n# Wire up the inverse transform so FlareLightningModule applies it before every model call.\\npreprocess_fn = partial(\\n    destandardize_channels,\\n    channel_order=cfg.data.channels,\\n    scalers=scalers,\\n)\\n\\nlit_model = FlareLightningModule(\\n    model, metrics, lr=cfg.learning_rate, batch_size=batch_size, preprocess_fn=preprocess_fn\\n)\\n\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"553565e7\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"## Logging\\n\\nIn order to properly compare experiments against each other, it is very useful to log evaluation metrics in a place where they can be compared against other training runs.  In this workshop we will use Weights and Biases (WandB). \\n\\nThe first time you run WandB in a machine it will ask you to login to WandB.  You should have received an invitation to our project.  In order to login you must:\\n\\n- Select option 2 (existing account).   In VScode the dialog opens a box at the top of your screen.\\n- Click on get API Key (this will open a browser).\\n- Generate API Key.\\n- Paste it in the dialog box at the top of your VSCode\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"7b9c76c8\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"project_name = cfg.wandb_project\\nrun_name = \\\"baseline_experiment_1\\\"  # give your run a descriptive name\\n\\nwandb_logger = WandbLogger(\\n    entity=cfg.wandb_entity,  # set wandb_entity in the config; null = personal account\\n    project=project_name,\\n    name=run_name,\\n    log_model=False,\\n    save_dir=\\\"./wandb/wandb_tmp\\\",\\n)\\n\\ncsv_logger = CSVLogger(\\\"runs\\\", name=project_name)\\n\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"48c4d138\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"## Initialize trainer\\n\\nWith the loggers done, now the trainer needs to be defined.  The trainer defines several properties of your training run. Here we define:\\n\\n- The max number of epochs (one epoch represents your model seeing your entire training dataset).\\n- Define where the training run will take place (auto uses the GPU if possible, if not, CPU).\\n- The loggers.\\n- The callbacks (here we save the model with the lowest validation loss).\\n- Logging frequency (because we are working with a small dataset it needs to be small).\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"7e24e547\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"max_epochs = 2\\n\\n# -------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n# Trainer\\n# -------------------------------------------------------------------------\\ntrainer = L.Trainer(\\n    max_epochs=max_epochs,\\n    accelerator=\\\"auto\\\",\\n    devices=\\\"auto\\\",\\n    logger=[wandb_logger, csv_logger],\\n    callbacks=[\\n        ModelCheckpoint(\\n            monitor=\\\"val_loss\\\",\\n            mode=\\\"min\\\",\\n            save_top_k=1,\\n        )\\n    ],\\n    log_every_n_steps=2,\\n)\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"b008c604\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"## Fit the model\\n\\nFinally we fit the model.  We pass the Lighting module, and our dataloaders.\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"1088d108\"},{\"cellType\":\"code\",\"source\":\"trainer.fit(lit_model, train_data_loader, val_data_loader)\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"f8a6e448\",\"language\":\"python\"},{\"cellType\":\"markdown\",\"source\":\"## Conclusion\\n\\nWith this we have now integrated our dataset, dataloaders, metrics, and baseline into an end-2-end training loop.  The next step is to substitute the simple model with Surya.\",\"execution_count\":null,\"cell_id\":\"ebcc1a23\"}]}",
            "kind": "other"
          }
        ]
      },
      "raw_time": false,
      "time": 1789570257.737928
    },
