      "type": "msg",
      "body": "Found it \u2014 cell 24 (`train_loss_metrics(output, batch[\"forecast\"])`) fails because of a real shape contract violation between two files, not because of anything in the notebook itself:\n\n- `sf_triggers_metrics.py` (`FlareMetrics`) is explicitly documented: *\"predictions arrive as (B,) ... targets are always (B, 1)\"* \u2014 i.e. this metrics code, and the whole pipeline (the real `HelioSpectformer1D` model is built with `num_outputs=1`, a scalar), expects a **scalar** target per sample.\n- But `FlareDSDataset.__getitem__` in `sf_triggers_dataset.py` sets `sample[\"forecast\"] = self._load_mask(mask_path)`, which is the raw SHARP bitmap \u2014 a **(1, 4096, 4096) spatial mask**, not a scalar. That's why `output` (shape `(2,)`) and `batch[\"forecast\"]` (shape `(2, 1, 4096, 4096)`, i.e. 33,554,432 elements) can't broadcast.\n\nSo the dataset is producing a segmentation-style label while the rest of the app (model, metrics, notebook) is built for scalar regression. This is a design mismatch, not a typo, and there are two legitimate ways to resolve it:\n\n1. **Reduce the mask to a scalar** in `FlareDSDataset` (e.g. fraction of active pixels in the SHARP bitmap, or some other summary statistic) \u2014 keeps the linear baseline, `HelioSpectformer1D(num_outputs=1)`, and `FlareMetrics`'s documented contract all unchanged.\n2. **Treat this as a genuine spatial task** \u2014 keep the full-resolution mask as target, but then the linear baseline model, `HelioSpectformer1D` output head, and `FlareMetrics` all need to become spatial (this is a much bigger change, and contradicts the \"simple regression baseline\" framing in the notebook's own markdown).\n\nGiven `num_outputs=1` is baked into the real model builder (`3_finetune_template_1D.py`) and the metrics docstring, I'd guess (1) is what you intended and the mask-loading is just not yet reduced to the scalar label. Which is it \u2014 do you want `forecast` reduced to a scalar (and if so, what should that scalar represent \u2014 e.g. mask coverage fraction, or should it instead come from a flare-intensity column in the catalog like the `template` app's `_flare_label_transform` does), or is this genuinely meant to be a spatial mask-prediction task?",
      "raw_time": false
    },
    {
      "time": 1789572185.9722033,
      "metadata": {
        "tool_calls": [
          {
            "status": "completed",
            "title": "Working...",
            "raw_output": "1\t\"\"\"\n2\tTemplate metrics for flare forecasting.\n3\t\n4\tFlareMetrics defines four metric sets:\n5\t- \"train_loss\"    \u2014 differentiable loss that drives backpropagation (MSE).\n6\t- \"val_loss\"      \u2014 the quantity logged as `val_loss` and used to select checkpoints.\n7\t                    Defaults to the same MSE as \"train_loss\"; override it when your task\n8\t                    needs a different validation objective.\n9\t- \"train_metrics\" \u2014 non-differentiable metrics logged during training (RRSE).\n10\t- \"val_metrics\"   \u2014 metrics logged at validation for reporting only (MSE + RRSE). These\n11\t                    do NOT influence checkpoint selection \u2014 \"val_loss\" does.\n12\t\n13\tThe __call__ method selects the appropriate metric set based on the mode passed at\n14\tconstruction time. The dictionary keys returned by each method become the metric names\n15\tpropagated to the logger (e.g. WandB, CSV).\n16\t\"\"\"\n17\t\n18\timport torch\n19\timport torchmetrics as tm  # Lots of possible metrics in here https://lightning.ai/docs/torchmetrics/stable/all-metrics.html\n20\t\n21\t# Shape contract: predictions arrive as (B,) from HelioSpectformer1D or (B, 1) from the\n22\t# linear baseline, while targets are always (B, 1). Every metric below flattens both with\n23\t# reshape(-1) rather than squeeze(-1): squeeze is shape-dependent and collapses a\n24\t# batch of one to a 0-d scalar, which then fails to broadcast against a (1,) target.\n25\tclass FlareMetrics:\n26\t    def __init__(self, mode: str):\n27\t        \"\"\"\n28\t        Initialize FlareMetrics class.\n29\t\n30\t        Args:\n31\t            mode (str): Mode to use for metric evaluation. One of \"train_loss\",\n32\t                        \"val_loss\", \"train_metrics\", or \"val_metrics\".\n33\t        \"\"\"\n34\t        self.mode = mode\n35\t\n36\t        # Cache torchmetrics instances once (instead of recreating each call)\n37\t        self._rrse = tm.RelativeSquaredError(squared=False)\n38\t\n39\t    def _ensure_device(self, preds: torch.Tensor) -> None:\n40\t        \"\"\"Move torchmetrics modules to the same device as ``preds``, if needed.\"\"\"\n41\t        if self._rrse.device != preds.device:\n42\t            self._rrse = self._rrse.to(preds.device)\n43\t\n44\t    def train_loss(\n45\t        self, preds: torch.Tensor, target: torch.Tensor\n46\t    ) -> tuple[dict[str, torch.Tensor], list[float]]:\n47\t        \"\"\"\n48\t        Calculate loss metrics for training.\n49\t\n50\t        Args:\n51\t            preds (torch.Tensor): Model predictions.\n52\t            target (torch.Tensor): Ground truth labels.\n53\t\n54\t        Returns:\n55\t            tuple[dict[str, torch.Tensor], list[float]]:\n56\t                - dict[str, torch.Tensor]: Dictionary containing the calculated loss metrics.\n57\t                                        Keys are metric names (e.g., \"mse\"), and values are the\n58\t                                        corresponding torch.Tensor values.\n59\t                - list[float]: List of weights for each calculated metric.\n60\t        \"\"\"\n61\t\n62\t        output_metrics = {}\n63\t        output_weights = []\n64\t\n65\t        output_metrics[\"mse\"] = torch.nn.functional.mse_loss(preds.reshape(-1), target.reshape(-1))\n66\t        output_weights.append(1)\n67\t\n68\t        return output_metrics, output_weights\n69\t\n70\t    def val_loss(\n71\t        self, preds: torch.Tensor, target: torch.Tensor\n72\t    ) -> tuple[dict[str, torch.Tensor], list[float]]:\n73\t        \"\"\"\n74\t        Calculate the validation loss \u2014 the quantity logged as ``val_loss`` and used by\n75\t        ModelCheckpoint to select the best model.\n76\t\n77\t        By default this delegates to ``train_loss``, so the monitored quantity has the same\n78\t        form as the training objective and the two cannot drift apart by accident. This is\n79\t        the hook to override when your task needs a different validation objective (a\n80\t        different weighting, a metric that is meaningful only on held-out data, etc.).\n81\t\n82\t        Note that this is deliberately separate from ``val_metrics``: those are reported\n83\t        for information only and do not affect checkpoint selection.\n84\t\n85\t        Args:\n86\t            preds (torch.Tensor): Model predictions.\n87\t            target (torch.Tensor): Ground truth labels.\n88\t\n89\t        Returns:\n90\t            tuple[dict[str, torch.Tensor], list[float]]:\n91\t                - dict[str, torch.Tensor]: Dictionary containing the calculated loss metrics.\n92\t                - list[float]: List of weights for each calculated metric.\n93\t        \"\"\"\n94\t        return self.train_loss(preds, target)\n95\t\n96\t    def train_metrics(\n97\t        self, preds: torch.Tensor, target: torch.Tensor\n98\t    ) -> tuple[dict[str, torch.Tensor], list[float]]:\n99\t        \"\"\"\n100\t        Calculate evaluation metrics for training.\n101\t        IMPORTANT:  These metrics are only for reporting purposes and do not\n102\t                    contribute to the training loss. Use only if you want to\n103\t                    monitor additional metrics during training.\n104\t\n105\t        Args:\n106\t            preds (torch.Tensor): Model predictions.\n107\t            target (torch.Tensor): Ground truth labels.\n108\t\n109\t        Returns:\n110\t            tuple[dict[str, torch.Tensor], list[float]]:\n111\t                - dict[str, torch.Tensor]: Dictionary containing the calculated evaluation metrics.\n112\t                                        Keys are metric names, and values are the corresponding torch.Tensor values.\n113\t                - list[float]: List of weights for each calculated metric.\n114\t        \"\"\"\n115\t        output_metrics = {}\n116\t        output_weights = []\n117\t\n118\t        self._ensure_device(preds)\n119\t        output_metrics[\"rrse\"] = self._rrse(preds.reshape(-1), target.reshape(-1))\n120\t        output_weights.append(1)        \n121\t\n122\t\n123\t        return output_metrics, output_weights\n124\t\n125\t    def val_metrics(\n126\t        self, preds: torch.Tensor, target: torch.Tensor\n127\t    ) -> tuple[dict[str, torch.Tensor], list[float]]:\n128\t        \"\"\"\n129\t        Calculate metrics for validation.\n130\t\n131\t        Args:\n132\t            preds (torch.Tensor): Model predictions.\n133\t            target (torch.Tensor): Ground truth labels.\n134\t\n135\t        Returns:\n136\t            tuple[dict[str, torch.Tensor], list[float]]:\n137\t                - dict[str, torch.Tensor]: Dictionary containing the calculated metrics.\n138\t                                        Keys are metric names (e.g., \"mse\"), and values are the\n139\t                                        corresponding torch.Tensor values.\n140\t                - list[float]: List of weights for each calculated metric.\n141\t        \"\"\"\n142\t\n143\t        output_metrics = {}\n144\t        output_weights = []\n145\t\n146\t        output_metrics[\"mse\"] = torch.nn.functional.mse_loss(preds.reshape(-1), target.reshape(-1))\n147\t        output_weights.append(1)\n148\t\n149\t        self._ensure_device(preds)\n150\t        output_metrics[\"rrse\"] = self._rrse(preds.reshape(-1), target.reshape(-1))\n151\t        output_weights.append(1)            \n152\t\n153\t        return output_metrics, output_weights\n154\t\n155\t    def __call__(\n156\t        self, preds: torch.Tensor, target: torch.Tensor\n157\t    ) -> tuple[dict[str, torch.Tensor], list[float]]:\n158\t        \"\"\"Evaluate metrics for the mode set at construction time.\n159\t\n160\t        Args:\n161\t            preds: Model output tensor. Shape depends on the application.\n162\t            target: Ground truth tensor to compare against.\n163\t\n164\t        Returns:\n165\t            tuple[dict[str, torch.Tensor], list[float]]:\n166\t                - Metric dictionary. Keys become logger metric names; values are\n167\t                  scalar tensors aggregated over the batch.\n168\t                - List of per-metric weights (used by FlareLightningModule to\n169\t                  combine multiple loss terms into a single scalar).\n170\t        \"\"\"\n171\t\n172\t        match self.mode.lower():\n173\t\n174\t            case \"train_loss\":\n175\t                return self.train_loss(preds, target)\n176\t\n177\t            # No torch.no_grad() here, matching \"train_loss\": Lightning already disables\n178\t            # gradients during validation, so wrapping it would differ gratuitously from\n179\t            # the loss case this mirrors.\n180\t            case \"val_loss\":\n181\t                return self.val_loss(preds, target)\n182\t\n183\t            case \"train_metrics\":\n184\t                with torch.no_grad():\n185\t                    return self.train_metrics(preds, target)\n186\t\n187\t            case \"val_metrics\":\n188\t                with torch.no_grad():\n189\t                    return self.val_metrics(preds, target)\n190\t\n191\t            case _:\n192\t                raise NotImplementedError(\n193\t                    f\"{self.mode} is not implemented as a valid metric case.\"\n194\t                )\n195\t",
            "tool_call_id": "toolu_bdrk_01F3gFf7xhyw4yqi8AUU7Nj8"
          }
        ]
      },
